  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 245 AM MST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nThe next upper level low will traverse across the region in the \nFriday or Friday night time period, but models are all over the \nplace concerning what to do with this system. However, all scenarios \nare fairly low impact regardless of the exact track of the upper \nlevel low, which differs from just north of our area all the way to \nnorthern Mexico. About 25% of ensemble members (mostly from the\nGEFS) move the compact upper level low through our area, which \nwould bring the potential for some light precipitation on \nFriday. Low end PoPs (15 to 20%) were introduced with this \nupdate to account for this possibility. With this bit of \nunsettled weather around, temperatures should remain fairly \nclose to or slightly above seasonal averages Friday and \nSaturday. \n\nOnce that system moves through the area, models are in fairly good \nagreement showing clean ridging building back into the area. NAEFS \nmean 700-mb temperatures climb to the +6 to +8C range over much of \nthe area by Sunday, above the climatological 90th percentile. \nTemperatures are expected to surge well above normal for Sunday, and \nremain there into the early part of next week. Look for highs \ngenerally in the 60s east of the Laramie range, and mid to upper 50s \nto the west. These values are about 10F above average. Precipitation \nchances look to remain minimal during this period. There is a slim \nchances (about 25%) for some elevated winds Sunday night or Monday \nas the ridge flattens out and the jet stream gets slightly closer to \nthe area.",
